  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe 00Z RAOB sampling and WV imagery show a strong ridge over the \nFour Corners with its axis extending into the central Plains. The \nWesterlies remain displaced generally across the northern Plains and \nsouthern Canada. The Great Lakes trough drifts east while the \nPacific trough digs into the Pacific Northwest. Mid level WV over \nKansas is generally dry with overall subsidence in place. The \nmonsoonal moist plume continues to stream across central California \ninto the northern Plains. Surface high pressure ridge has slid into \nthe Ohio Valley, with lee troughing continuing to deepen over \neastern Colorado and western Kansas with low level flow returning \nfrom the south. The warm advection regime is underway for more warm \nand humid conditions.\n\nToday, south winds reach to around 15 to 20 mph with gusts \napproaching 30 mph by afternoon and stay gusty into Monday as mid \nlevel southwesterly flow strengthen. H85 temperatures climb from the\nmiddle 20s C today to the upper 20s C Sunday. Forecast soundings \ncontinue to exhibit inverted V structure with a deep dry mixed layer,\nand that mixing caps dewpoints in the low to middle 60s while highs \nreach the middle to upper 90s. Peak heat indices land near 100, \nhighest Sunday and Monday. Triple digits may be reached for highs \nacross portions of north central Kansas, likely at Concordia Sunday \nand still possible Monday, with the Kansas River valley areas a few \ndegrees cooler. Warm overnight lows give Topeka and Concordia a \ndecent chance of holding near 80 Monday morning against a record warm\nlow in the upper 70s, and several records are in play through \nTuesday. Heat indices continue to remain just shy of advisory \ncriteria, so no headlines for now. Duration into a fifth and sixth \nday is what we will be the primary potential hazard but acclimation \nto the heat this late in the season is high.\n\nThe ridge flattens early next week. Mid level heights generally \nlower through Tuesday as the Pacific trough ejects across the \nnorthern Plains, and the trailing front sags toward the Nebraska \nborder Sunday night into Monday and effectively washes out. \nEnsembles keep trending drier across the area with the weak areas of \nascent remaining north of the area with with minor shortwaves \nrippling through the upper flow. So, expect rain chances hold very \nlow through Tuesday and no meaningful relief from the weak boundary.\nThe ridge amplifies later in the week and highs stay in the middle \n90s with heat indices near 100 through at least Thursday. Any storms \nwould ride the northern edge of the ridge as mesoscale systems, and \nslight chances Thursday and Friday over far northern Kansas cover \nthat. High confidence in the temperature trend, low confidence in any\none day of storms.\n\nEnsemble height spreads widen beyond Thursday, and the NBM trends \ncooler with highs back into the lower 90s Saturday and upper 80s by \nSunday and Labor Day with lows possibly back in the 60s. Any \nmeaningful precipitation chance remains to be seen at this point.",
